--- log ---
2026-09-08T10:15Z claude created — filed from a Fable-model UX consult requested by the author (pause/unpause switch question, launch-dialog clarity question). Full analysis below is Fable's, lightly reorganized into ticket sections; verdict and reasoning are Fable's own, grounded in a read of bootstrap.js, scheduler.js, SPEC.md R22, the 2026-09-05 DECISIONS ratifications, and the closed/open sitter dialog tickets — not a cold take from the title alone. Label needs-human because the recommendation requires amending which control counts as R22's "one obvious way", a ratification-level call this ticket does not have standing to make.

--- body ---
## The two questions asked

1. Should the sitter's dialog carry an explicit pause/unpause switch, distinct
   from add-on disable?
2. Is the launch confirmation dialog clear and well-behaved, and if not, what
   should change?

## TL;DR (Fable's verdict)

1. No separate pause toggle. Turn the existing launch answer into the one
   persisted on/off switch the dialog carries — that switch IS the pause, and
   it is the only one the plugin should have.
2. The launch confirmation is not well-behaved: synchronous modal at every
   Zotero start, answer forgotten at restart and at disable/re-enable,
   "tonight?" mis-describes a loop that runs forever, "No" is a dead end
   recoverable only through the Add-ons manager. Fix: ask once, persisted
   pref, labelled buttons, move disclosures into the dialog.

## Question 1 — why not a third control

The plugin already has two stop mechanisms, and that is the problem:

- **Add-on disable** (ratified 2026-09-05, SPEC.md R22's named control):
  graceful, holds across restarts, but lives four clicks away in Tools ->
  Add-ons, removes the button/dialog that would show it stopped, and
  re-enable restarts the census from scratch and re-asks the launch question
  (confirmed live, ticket 0727 arm 4, 2026-09-08).
- **Declining the launch prompt**: session-only, nothing persisted, next
  Zotero start asks again. Leaves the toolbar reading "Index" with tooltip
  "Not started: turn the add-on off, then on again" and the dialog showing
  "Files indexed: 0 / No indexing under way" with no control on it.

Adding a third, in-dialog "Pause" would collide with vocabulary already on
screen: `phase-cpu-busy`, `phase-low-memory`, `phase-low-disk` all render as
"Paused: ..." (bootstrap.js:114-118). A user seeing "Paused: processor busy"
next to a Pause button will look for how to unpause something they did not
pause.

R22 itself requires "one obvious way ... and it MUST hold across restarts."
A session-only pause fails the restart clause; a second persisted switch
fails the "one" clause. The design that satisfies both: collapse the launch
decision and the pause into one persisted state, replacing the launch
prompt's role.

- **Off** — no sweep scheduled, no census (R22 covers all background work,
  and the census hashes files). In-flight `ensure()` finishes — the same
  graceful semantics already ratified for disable. Toolbar reads a plain
  "Indexing off"; dialog's first line offers the one control to turn it on.
- **On** — current behaviour.

Alternatives considered and rejected: a session-only pause button (violates
R22's restart clause); keeping the modal and adding pause as a third control
(three controls, three persistence behaviours); leaving disable as the only
switch (defensible only if the launch prompt stops acting as a de facto
second switch — fixing question 2 already builds this persisted state, so
the toggle costs one line of UI on top).

## Question 2 — the launch confirmation's concrete defects

What the user meets today (bootstrap.js:1388-1392): a `Services.prompt.confirm`
— synchronous OS modal, OK/Cancel — fired from `initialize()` right after
`uiReadyPromise`, at EVERY Zotero start. Title "Indexing assistant —
experimental", ~90 words across four paragraphs (the question, resource
conditions, worker-non-preemption disclosure, disable semantics).

1. **Fires every startup.** No pref records the answer; unrelated to
   yesterday's choice. Confirmed to also fire on disable/re-enable (0727
   arm 4).
2. **"Index the whole library tonight?" is false twice.** Asked at whatever
   hour Zotero opens, and the sweep loop reschedules for the session's whole
   life — nothing ends at dawn. "Whole library" — unscoped census (0717
   already tracks this surface).
3. **Decline is a dead end.** `phase-launch-declined` points the user to the
   Add-ons manager; the button/dialog stay installed but inert (`alive` is
   true, pulse never starts, dialog doesn't redraw). A "No" undoable only
   outside the plugin is a trap.
4. **OK/Cancel don't answer the question asked.** `confirmEx` allows labelled
   buttons ("Start indexing" / "Not now") instead.
5. **Disclosure buried in a consent box.** The 2026-09-05 worker-limitation
   disclosure must stay, but a four-paragraph confirm is the format least
   likely to be read. Belongs in the dialog (0693's Details layer), with the
   first-run question carrying one short sentence and a pointer.
6. **Ordering race.** `alive = true` and the toolbar button are installed
   BEFORE the confirm (bootstrap.js:1383-1385), so the button is visible
   under a modal saying the thing may not start — the same class of defect
   0696 had to guard against.

## Rough build shape (Fable's sketch, not a committed design)

- One tri-state pref (unset / on / off), read in `initialize()`. Unset -> ask
  once via `confirmEx` with labelled buttons, then write the answer. Never
  ask again.
- Off is a real, persisted state: toolbar "Indexing off"; dialog layer 1
  shows the switch; no census runs.
- Move `launch-worker` / `launch-disable` strings into 0693's Details layer;
  shrink `launch-question`, drop "tonight".
- Ask before arming: move the confirm before `alive = true` / button
  installation, closing the ordering race.
- `scheduler.js`'s existing `enabled` flag covers most of on/off; `stop()`
  needs a resumable twin or a fresh `createSDTSitter` call.
- Tests to update: `tests/test_sdt_sitter.py:715-723` pins the four string
  ids at the confirm site; `sdt_sitter_zotero_mock.mjs` needs an
  `options.launch`; 0727 arm 4's "re-asks on re-enable" observation becomes
  the red fixture for the persistence claim.
- Paperwork: a DECISIONS.md entry amending which control is R22's "one
  obvious way" (add-on disable keeps its graceful host-level semantics; the
  plugin's own persisted switch becomes the R22 control); SPEC.md's sitter
  paragraph edited in the same PR; 0691's vocabulary table gains "off/on"
  and a rename of the automatic gate labels from "Paused:" to "Waiting:" (so
  "on/off" stays reserved for the user's own switch); 0717's launch-prompt
  surface narrows to the shortened first-run text.

Composes with 0693 (switch lives in layer 1, disclosures in layer 2) rather
than reopening it; repeats nothing 0687/0684/0696 already tried or reverted.

## Relevant files

- `plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js` — `initialize()` (1129-1396), the confirm
  (1388-1392), the phase table (489-503), `shutdown()` (1397)
- `plugins/sdt-sitter/scheduler.js` — `stop()`, the `enabled` flag
- `SPEC.md` — R22 (line 617) and its sitter design paragraph (2241-2258)
- `DECISIONS.md` — the 2026-09-05 ratifications (6594-6630)
- `tests/test_sdt_sitter.py:715-723` — pins the confirm-site string ids
- Closed: 0680, 0684, 0685, 0687, 0691, 0694, 0696, 0727 (design history —
  read before proposing anything, don't repeat a reverted attempt)
- Open, composes with: 0693 (dialog layers), 0717 (launch-prompt copy)

## Actions

1. **Author ruling first** (this is why the ticket is `needs-human`): decide
   whether the plugin's own persisted switch becomes R22's "one obvious way",
   with add-on disable remaining the host-level graceful control. Record the
   ruling in DECISIONS.md before any code changes land.
2. Once ruled: implement the persisted tri-state pref, the once-only labelled
   first-run question, the ordering fix (ask before arming), and the on/off
   dialog control per the build shape above.
3. Move the worker-limitation and disable-semantics disclosures into 0693's
   Details layer.
4. Update SPEC.md's sitter paragraph and 0691's vocabulary table
   ("Paused:" -> "Waiting:" for automatic gates) in the same change.

## Test

- Red first: a fresh profile's first activation asks once and persists the
  answer; a second activation in a new session does not ask again.
- Red first: disabling then re-enabling the add-on (0727 arm 4's scenario)
  does not re-ask the launch question if a persisted answer already exists.
- Red first: declining leaves a real, discoverable "off" state reachable
  from the dialog, not only from the Add-ons manager.
- Mixed-population trap to watch: a test that only exercises the "on" path
  cannot see a regression that stops "off" from ever completing a clean
  shutdown of in-flight `ensure()` work.

## Verification

- [ ] DECISIONS.md carries the author's ruling on R22's "one obvious way"
      before implementation is merged
- [ ] The launch question is asked at most once per profile and its answer
      persists across restart and across disable/re-enable
- [ ] "Off" is a discoverable, single-switch state inside the dialog, not
      only reachable via Add-ons manager
- [ ] The worker-limitation and disable-semantics disclosures are readable
      in the dialog's Details layer at any time, not gated behind a modal
- [ ] The ordering race (button installed before confirm) is closed
- [ ] `make check` passes

## Invariants

- No change to the disposable-cache ruling: a pref recording on/off is not
  a job or failure ledger.
- No change to the graceful-stop semantics: in-flight `ensure()` still
  finishes on either disable or the new "off" state.
- The plugin still never writes to `storage/`, never changes extraction
  settings, never replaces a source attachment.

## Exit criteria

The author has ruled on which control is R22's "one obvious way"; the launch
prompt asks at most once and its answer persists across every transition
tested in 0727; "off" is a real, single, discoverable state; disclosures are
readable outside the modal; and the tests above are red-first and green.
